export const QUESTION_CATALOG = [{"id":1,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement best captures the course rule of נ״ט בר נ״ט?","choices":["Meat/dairy taste can become weakened through successive transfers; that leniency is specific to בשר בחלב and does not neutralize an inherently forbidden taste.","Any taste becomes permitted after two transfers, whether it began as meat, dairy, or an inherently forbidden substance.","נ״ט בר נ״ט applies only when both original foods are parve.","The halacha rejects Shmuel and treats every transferred meat/dairy taste as fully strong."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":2,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"Two bowls of plain pasta were cooked in the same clean ben-yomo meat pot. In Bowl 1, no cheese has been added yet. In Bowl 2, cheese was already mixed in. Which statement is correct?","choices":["S”A and Rama both forbid adding cheese to Bowl 1, and both forbid Bowl 2.","S”A permits adding cheese to Bowl 1; Rama says not to add it לכתחילה. Bowl 2 is permitted even according to the Rama.","S”A permits Bowl 2 only בדיעבד, while the Rama permits adding cheese to Bowl 1 לכתחילה.","The status of both bowls depends only on whether the cheese itself is ben-yomo."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":3,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"Plain potatoes were cooked in a clean eino-ben-yomo dairy pot. You now want to serve them with meat. According to the course practical rule, what may you do?","choices":["Only S”A permits it, and only בדיעבד.","Only the Rama permits it לכתחילה.","Both S”A and Rama forbid combining them with meat.","Both S”A and Rama permit combining them with meat לכתחילה."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":4,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"You deliberately want to cook parve noodles now in a clean ben-yomo dairy pot specifically so that you can serve them with meat. Which statement best matches the course positions?","choices":["Within S”A practice the issue is disputed and Rav Ovadya permits; under the Rama it is forbidden לכתחילה.","S”A practice is unanimous that it is forbidden, while the Rama permits.","Rav Ovadya forbids, while both S”A practice and the Rama are otherwise uniformly permissive.","Everyone permits because the noodles themselves are parve."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":5,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"An Ashkenazi has no other pot available and wants to cook parve eggs in a clean eino-ben-yomo dairy pot specifically for a meat egg salad. Which answer reflects the course dispute?","choices":["Gra/Badei HaShulchan forbid even when no other pot exists, while Chochmat Adam/Rav Elyashiv always permit.","Gra/Badei HaShulchan permit; Chochmat Adam/Rav Elyashiv forbid initially but allow when no other pot is available.","All of the course authorities forbid, because the pot is dairy.","All of the course authorities permit only after the eggs have already been mixed with meat."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":6,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all that apply. Which situations are permitted under the course נ״ט בר נ״ט rules?","choices":["Following the taught practical rule and Rav Moshe, place dry parve food cooked in a ben-yomo meat vessel onto a clean dairy plate, while recognizing the Pri Megadim dissent.","Mix parve food cooked in a ben-yomo meat pot with parve food cooked in a ben-yomo dairy pot.","Under the Rama, intentionally add dairy לכתחילה to parve food already cooked in a ben-yomo meat pot.","Rely on נ״ט בר נ״ט to neutralize non-kosher taste carried by recycled processing water."],"answer":["A","B"]},{"id":7,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"Cheese was cooked with a pepper, and the pepper was later fried in a parve pan. Which statement matches the course food → food → vessel dispute?","choices":["Pri Megadim/Aruch HaShulchan count the first food-to-food transfer as נ״ט בר נ״ט, while Pnei Aryeh/Rav Ovadya do not.","Pri Megadim/Aruch HaShulchan do not treat the first food-to-food transfer as נ״ט בר נ״ט; Pnei Aryeh/Rav Ovadya do; Chavot Da’at/Yad Yehuda are intermediate.","All of the course authorities agree that every two transfers count as נ״ט בר נ״ט.","All of the course authorities agree that a transfer must always begin in a כלי."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":8,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"Milk is on the outside of a pot while parve potatoes are still cooking inside, so the transfer occurs while source and recipient remain connected. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Chavot Da’at and Beit Ephraim both say it is definitely נ״ט בר נ״ט from the start.","Beit Ephraim says it is not נ״ט בר נ״ט, while Chavot Da’at is lenient; Ashkenazim follow the lenient view לכתחילה.","Chavot Da’at says it is not yet נ״ט בר נ״ט; Beit Ephraim says it can be. Ashkenazim are initially cautious but can rely on Beit Ephraim בדיעבד or for significant loss, and Rav Ovadya follows the lenient view.","Rav Ovadya follows Chavot Da’at and forbids even בדיעבד."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":9,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"You fried a sharp parve onion mixture in an eino-ben-yomo dairy pan and now want to combine it with meat. Which statement best reflects the course דבר חריף rule?","choices":["The S”A track is more lenient with eino-ben-yomo, while the Rama treats sharp food as capable of reviving absorbed taste into actual meat/dairy status.","Both S”A and Rama treat sharp food exactly like ordinary parve food in every case.","The Rama is lenient only because the pan is eino-ben-yomo, and sharpness never affects absorbed taste.","Sharp food is relevant only when the pan is ben-yomo; eino-ben-yomo cases are never disputed."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":10,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"On Shabbos morning you warm one piece of challah directly on a dry meat cholent-pot lid and a second piece in foil on that same dry lid. You later want to eat both with dairy. Which answer matches the course rules?","choices":["The directly warmed challah tracks the ongoing-connection dispute and, under the Rama, should not intentionally be eaten with dairy; the foil-wrapped challah may be eaten with dairy.","Both pieces may be eaten with dairy according to every course position.","The foil-wrapped challah is stricter because the foil adds another hot surface.","Both pieces are forbidden because anything placed on a meat-pot lid becomes meat."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":11,"category":"נ״ט בר נ״ט","type":"single","prompt":"A sous-vide unit was used for dairy, then the water was emptied and the equipment was cleaned. Kosher meat was later cooked in it. Which statement matches the course treatment?","choices":["Ashkenazic practice permits one setup לכתחילה without any cleaning between uses.","Rav Ovadya requires separate equipment even בדיעבד.","Once meat was cooked, everyone treats it as automatically forbidden.","Ashkenazic practice prefers separate equipment, but gives room בדיעבד after cleaning; Rav Ovadya permits one setup when t